            <div xml:id="d346">

                <pb facs="0542" n="452" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg452"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.515/339: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-10-18T19:00:00-05:00">October 18, 1934—7
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>331. Your 473, October 18, 4 p.m., last sentence, in regard to export of silver.
                    Department will await receipt of Shanghai’s report which should contain a
                    detailed statement of the situation and, if possible, <pb facs="0543" n="453"
                        xml:id="pg453"/> an indication of the views of and the action, if any,
                    contemplated by the representatives of the other principally interested
                    powers.</p>
                <p>In the absence of further instructions, Department does not desire that protest
                    in regard to this subject be lodged with the Chinese Government.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
